  "response": "# Verdict: REJECT\n\n**Overall Assessment**: \nWhile `TabsWidget` exhibits a seemingly well-structured ARIA skeleton in the DOM (`role=\"tablist\"`, `role=\"tab\"`, `role=\"tabpanel\"`, `aria-selected`, `aria-controls`, and `aria-labelledby`), the component contains critical interaction design failures that completely block keyboard-only and assistive technology users. Specifically, roving `tabIndex` is only half-implemented: inactive tabs are assigned `tabIndex=\"-1\"`, but arrow key listeners are missing, leaving inactive tabs physically unreachable by keyboard. Furthermore, the custom `onKeyDown` handler contains a fatal ID mismatch bug that sets `activeTab` to `-1` when a user presses Space or Enter, hiding all panels and breaking the widget.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**:\n- *Prediction*: Arrow key navigation (`ArrowLeft` / `ArrowRight`) will be missing, leaving roving tabindex incomplete.\n- *Outcome*: **Confirmed**. Inactive tabs have `tabIndex={-1}`, but only `Enter` and `Space` are handled in `handleTabKeyDown`.\n- *Prediction*: Tabpanel without focusable elements will lack `tabIndex=\"0\"`.\n- *Outcome*: **Confirmed**. Panel `<div role=\"tabpanel\">` has static `<p>` content and lacks `tabIndex={0}`.\n- *Prediction*: Enter/Space handlers might interfere with native `<button>` activation.\n- *Outcome*: **Confirmed & Exceeded**. Not only does it intercept native activation, it contains an ID lookup mismatch that sets `activeTab` to `-1` and wipes out all visible content.\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### Critical Findings (Blocks Access)\n\n#### 1. Roving Tabindex Implemented Without Arrow Key Navigation Locks Out Inactive Tabs\n- **Evidence**: [`TabsWidget.jsx:27`](file:///Users/AlexUA_1/.gemini/antigravity-cli/scratch/TabsWidget.jsx#L27) (`tabIndex={index === activeTab ? 0 : -1}`) and [`TabsWidget.jsx:10-15`](file:///Users/AlexUA_1/.gemini/antigravity-cli/scratch/TabsWidget.jsx#L10-L15) (`handleTabKeyDown`).\n- **Pattern Involved**: WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices Guide (APG) Tabs Pattern (Keyboard Interaction); WCAG 2.2 Criterion 2.1.1 Keyboard (Level A), Criterion 2.4.3 Focus Order (Level A).\n- **User Group Impacted**: Keyboard-only users, switch device users, screen reader users navigating by widget controls.\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**:\n  The component sets `tabIndex={-1}` on every inactive tab. Under browser tab-order semantics, elements with `tabIndex=\"-1\"` are excluded from sequential Tab navigation. In a compliant WAI-ARIA tablist, focus moves among tabs using `ArrowLeft` and `ArrowRight` (and `Home`/`End`). Because `handleTabKeyDown` does not handle arrow keys, pressing Tab skips past all inactive tabs, and pressing Arrow keys does nothing. Keyboard-only users are physically barred from focusing, inspecting, or activating tabs 2 through N.\n- **Fix**:\n  Implement arrow key navigation (`ArrowRight`, `ArrowLeft`, `Home`, `End`) inside `handleTabKeyDown`. When arrow keys are pressed, calculate the next index (with wrapping), set the active tab (or move focus ref), and call `.focus()` on the corresponding button DOM node.\n\n```yaml\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: tabs-roving-tabindex-missing-arrow-keys\nfingerprint: a7c8e901f4b23d5e\nsource: TabsWidget.jsx:27\nwcag_or_apg: WAI-ARIA APG Tabs Pattern, WCAG 2.2 SC 2.1.1 (Level A)\nsection_508_fpc_context: Section 508 502.3 / WCAG 2.0 SC 2.1.1\nseverity: CRITICAL\nperspective_alarms: keyboard=HIGH, screen_reader=HIGH, low_vision=LOW, cognitive=MEDIUM\nevidence: \"TabsWidget.jsx:27 (tabIndex={index === activeTab ? 0 : -1}), TabsWidget.jsx:10-15 (handleTabKeyDown only checks Enter and Space)\"\nreproduction_steps:\n  - Mount TabsWidget with 2 or more tabs\n  - Press Tab to navigate into the tablist (focus arrives on active tab)\n  - Press ArrowRight or ArrowLeft\n  - Press Tab again\nexpected_behavior: ArrowRight/ArrowLeft moves focus to adjacent tabs per WAI-ARIA APG Tabs specification.\nactual_behavior: Arrow keys do nothing; Tab moves focus entirely out of the tablist, rendering tabs 2..N completely unreachable by keyboard.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Enter/Space Keydown Handler Destroys Component State and Hides All Panels Due to ID Mismatch\n- **Evidence**: [`TabsWidget.jsx:10-15`](file:///Users/AlexUA_1/.gemini/antigravity-cli/scratch/TabsWidget.jsx#L10-L15), specifically line 13:\n  ```jsx\n  handleTabClick(tabs.findIndex(t => t.id === e.currentTarget.id));\n  ```\n  compared against line 23:\n  ```jsx\n  id={`tab-${tab.id}`}\n  ```\n- **Pattern Involved**: WCAG 2.2 Criterion 2.1.1 Keyboard (Level A), Criterion 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A).\n- **User Group Impacted**: Keyboard-only users, screen reader users.\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**:\n  Each tab button has an `id` attribute of `\"tab-\" + tab.id` (e.g., `\"tab-profile\"`). In `handleTabKeyDown`, `t.id === e.currentTarget.id` compares raw `tab.id` (`\"profile\"`) with the DOM element `id` (`\"tab-profile\"`). This comparison is **always false**. `findIndex` returns `-1`, calling `setActiveTab(-1)`.\n  When `activeTab` becomes `-1`:\n  1. All tabs receive `aria-selected=\"false\"` and `tabIndex=\"-1\"`.\n  2. All panels receive `hidden={true}` (`display: none !important`).\n  3. All panels disappear from the screen and accessibility tree.\n  4. Because all buttons now have `tabIndex=\"-1\"`, the user cannot re-enter the tablist via Tab once focus blurs.\n  Furthermore, native `<button>` elements already dispatch `click` events upon pressing `Enter` or `Space`. Intercepting them with `e.preventDefault()` suppresses standard button behavior and replaces it with this destructive state corruption.\n- **Fix**:\n  Remove the `Enter` and `Space` interception from `handleTabKeyDown` entirely. Since the elements are native `<button>` tags, the browser automatically triggers `onClick={() => handleTabClick(index)}` when `Enter` or `Space` is pressed.\n\n```yaml\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: tabs-keydown-id-mismatch-state-crash\nfingerprint: b9d21054a8e6320c\nsource: TabsWidget.jsx:13\nwcag_or_apg: WCAG 2.2 SC 2.1.1 (Level A), WCAG 2.2 SC 4.1.2 (Level A)\nsection_508_fpc_context: Section 508 502.3 / WCAG 2.0 SC 2.1.1, SC 4.1.2\nseverity: CRITICAL\nperspective_alarms: keyboard=HIGH, screen_reader=HIGH, low_vision=LOW, cognitive=HIGH\nevidence: \"TabsWidget.jsx:13 (t.id === e.currentTarget.id fails because id is `tab-${tab.id}`)\"\nreproduction_steps:\n  - Focus the active tab using keyboard Tab\n  - Press Enter or Spacebar\nexpected_behavior: Tab remains active and selected; panel content remains visible.\nactual_behavior: findIndex evaluates to -1, activeTab is set to -1, all panels hide, and all tabs receive tabIndex=\"-1\", corrupting UI state.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n---\n\n### Major Findings (Significantly Degrades Experience)\n\n#### 1. Tabpanel with Static Content Lacks `tabIndex=\"0\"`\n- **Evidence**: [`TabsWidget.jsx:39-48`](file:///Users/AlexUA_1/.gemini/antigravity-cli/scratch/TabsWidget.jsx#L39-L48)\n  ```jsx\n  <div\n    key={tab.id}\n    id={`panel-${tab.id}`}\n    role=\"tabpanel\"\n    aria-labelledby={`tab-${tab.id}`}\n    hidden={index !== activeTab}\n    className={`tab-panel ${index === activeTab ? 'active' : ''}`}\n  >\n    <p>{tab.content}</p>\n  </div>\n  ```\n- **Pattern Involved**: WAI-ARIA APG Tabs Pattern (\"Tabpanel without focusable elements\"); WCAG 2.2 Criterion 2.1.1 Keyboard (Level A).\n- **User Group Impacted**: Keyboard-only users, screen reader users, screen magnification users.\n- **Confidence**: HIGH\n- **Why this matters**:\n  The tab panel contains only static text content (`<p>{tab.content}</p>`) without any focusable interactive descendants (such as links or buttons). Per WAI-ARIA APG guidelines, when a tabpanel contains no focusable elements, the element with `role=\"tabpanel\"` MUST have `tabIndex={0}` so that sequential keyboard navigation moves focus from the active tab directly into the panel. Without `tabIndex={0}`, pressing Tab from the tablist skips past the panel content entirely, stranding keyboard users and screen magnifier users who rely on focus to track reading position.\n- **Fix**:\n  Add `tabIndex={0}` to the active `<div role=\"tabpanel\">`:\n  ```jsx\n  <div\n    ...\n    role=\"tabpanel\"\n    tabIndex={0}\n    aria-labelledby={`tab-${tab.id}`}\n  >\n  ```\n\n```yaml\n### A11y Evidence Finding\nfinding_id: tabpanel-missing-tabindex-static-content\nfingerprint: c3e107f91845a672\nsource: TabsWidget.jsx:42\nwcag_or_apg: WAI-ARIA APG Tabs Pattern, WCAG 2.2 SC 2.1.1 (Level A)\nsection_508_fpc_context: Section 508 502.3 / WCAG 2.0 SC 2.1.1\nseverity: MAJOR\nperspective_alarms: keyboard=HIGH, screen_reader=MEDIUM, low_vision=LOW, cognitive=LOW\nevidence: \"TabsWidget.jsx:42 (<div role='tabpanel'> lacks tabIndex={0} while containing only non-interactive <p> text)\"\nreproduction_steps:\n  - Focus active tab\n  - Press Tab key to advance into the panel\nexpected_behavior: Focus lands on the active tabpanel container so keyboard users can navigate or scroll panel content.\nactual_behavior: Focus skips over the tabpanel entirely and exits the widget.\ntrend: new\n```\n\n---\n\n### Minor Findings (Friction But Workaround Exists)\n\n1. **Missing `Home` and `End` Keyboard Navigation**:\n   - **Evidence**: [`TabsWidget.jsx:10-15`](file:///Users/AlexUA_1/.gemini/antigravity-cli/scratch/TabsWidget.jsx#L10-L15).\n   - **Pattern**: WAI-ARIA APG Tabs Pattern.\n   - **User Group**: Keyboard-only users.\n   - **Impact**: In longer tab sets, users cannot quickly jump to the first tab via `Home` or the last tab via `End`.\n   - **Fix**: Add `Home` and `End` handling in the keydown listener to set focus to `0` and `tabs.length - 1` respectively.\n\n---\n\n### Enhancements (Best Practice Not Met)\n\n1. **Responsive / Reflow Wrapping on Tablist**:\n   - **Evidence**: `CSS:5-9` (`[role=\"tablist\"] { display: flex; ... }`).\n   - **Pattern**: WCAG 2.2 Criterion 1.4.10 Reflow (Level AA).\n   - **Impact**: At 200%\u2013400% zoom, tabs in a horizontal `display: flex` container without `flex-wrap: wrap` can cause horizontal scrollbars or clipping if the number of tabs exceeds the viewport width.\n   - **Fix**: Add `flex-wrap: wrap` or an accessible scroll container with directional indicators.\n\n2. **Explicit `aria-orientation`**:\n   - While `aria-orientation=\"horizontal\"` is the implicit default for `role=\"tablist\"`, declaring it explicitly aids maintainability and clarity if orientation ever switches dynamically.\n\n---\n\n## What's Missing\n\n1. **Missing DOM Focus Management**:\n   The component relies on React state (`activeTab`) to render `tabIndex=\"0\"` or `\"-1\"`, but contains no DOM focus coordination (`useRef` array or `e.currentTarget.focus()`). Roving tabindex requires programmatically focusing the newly activated tab element when navigating via arrow keys.\n2. **Missing Focusable Tabpanel Destination**:\n   Static panel content has no keyboard stop, breaking reading flow when navigating by keyboard.\n3. **Missing Arrow/Home/End Event Dispatchers**:\n   No `ArrowRight`, `ArrowLeft`, `Home`, or `End` key handling exists in the component.\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n- **Screen reader user (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver)**:\n  - The static semantics are correctly announced on initial load (\"Content tabs, tablist\", \"tab 1 of 3, selected\").\n  - However, screen reader users who use keyboard controls inside the widget will discover that inactive tabs cannot be reached via standard arrow navigation.\n  - If the user presses Enter/Space on the active tab, the ID mismatch immediately deselects all tabs, clears all panels, and leaves assistive technology without any selected state or visible panel content.\n- **Keyboard-only user**:\n  - **Catastrophic failure**: Inactive tabs have `tabIndex=\"-1\"`, and arrow navigation is unimplemented. Inactive tabs are 100% inaccessible.\n  - Pressing Space or Enter sets `activeTab` to `-1`, dropping keyboard focus from the tablist and hiding all content.\n  - Tabbing out of the active tab skips past the tabpanel text because the panel lacks `tabIndex=\"0\"`.\n- **Low vision user (200% zoom, high contrast)**:\n  - Color contrast is compliant: `#0066cc` on white provides a 5.56:1 contrast ratio (exceeds WCAG 4.5:1 text and 3:1 UI boundaries). `#666666` provides a 5.74:1 ratio.\n  - Focus indicator (`outline: 3px solid #0066cc; outline-offset: 2px`) is prominent and meets WCAG 2.4.7 and 2.4.13.\n  - Potential clipping or overflow issues at 400% zoom if `flex-wrap: wrap` is absent.\n- **Cognitive accessibility**:\n  - Content unexpectedly disappearing on Space/Enter causes high disorientation and gives the impression of a broken page or crash.\n- **Vestibular & motion**:\n  - Low risk. The component only uses a subtle color transition (`transition: color 0.2s`) and contains no motion/parallax triggers.\n- **Auditory access**:\n  - Low risk. No audio or multimedia dependencies.\n- **Environmental contrast**:\n  - Both active and inactive text colors exceed 4.5:1 against white backgrounds. Active state is reinforced with multiple visual cues (color, bold weight, 3px underline border).\n\n---\n\n### Perspective Alarm Levels\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | **HIGH** | Inactive tabs unreachable; Enter/Space wipes accessible state; panel unreferenced |\n| Keyboard-only | **HIGH** | Inactive tabs unreachable (roving tabindex without arrow keys); state crash on Enter/Space |\n| Low vision | **LOW** | Contrast ratios \u2265 5.5:1; clear 3px focus ring with offset |\n| Cognitive | **MEDIUM** | Entire content region vanishes when Enter/Space is pressed |\n| Vestibular & motion | **LOW** | No motion or animation beyond minor color transition |\n| Auditory access | **LOW** | No multimedia or auditory indicators |\n| Environmental contrast | **LOW** | Strong contrast ratios and multi-cue visual active indicator |\n\n---\n\n## Verdict Justification\n\n**Verdict: REJECT**\n\n- **Why this verdict**:\n  This component cannot be approved or shipped in its current state. Despite passing superficial automated linter rules (valid ARIA roles and matching attribute names), the component completely fails the fundamental requirements of WAI-ARIA APG Tabs:\n  1. Keyboard users are physically locked out of all inactive tabs (tabs 2..N) because `tabIndex={-1}` is set without the requisite arrow key listeners.\n  2. Pressing Space or Enter triggers a fatal ID mismatch bug that sets `activeTab` to `-1`, crashing the component state, hiding all content, and trapping focus.\n  3. The static tabpanel cannot be focused sequentially via Tab.\n\n- **Realist Check & Calibration**:\n  - *Could this be downgraded to MAJOR?* No. Inactive tabs are 100% inaccessible to keyboard users, and activating the current tab hides all UI content. This is a complete blockage of core functionality, not mere friction.\n  - *What is needed for ACCEPTANCE?*\n    1. Implement arrow key navigation (`ArrowLeft`, `ArrowRight`, `Home`, `End`) with DOM focus management.\n    2. Remove the broken `handleTabKeyDown` Enter/Space override and let native `<button>` click handling operate cleanly.\n    3. Add `tabIndex={0}` to the active tabpanel.\n\n---\n\n## Remediation Plan & Corrected Implementation\n\n```jsx\nimport React, { useState, useRef, useEffect } from 'react';\n\nconst TabsWidget = ({ tabs }) => {\n  const [activeTab, setActiveTab] = useState(0);\n  const tabRefs = useRef([]);\n\n  const handleTabClick = (index) => {\n    setActiveTab(index);\n  };\n\n  const handleTabKeyDown = (e, index) => {\n    let targetIndex = null;\n\n    switch (e.key) {\n      case 'ArrowRight':\n      case 'ArrowDown':\n        e.preventDefault();\n        targetIndex = (index + 1) % tabs.length;\n        break;\n      case 'ArrowLeft':\n      case 'ArrowUp':\n        e.preventDefault();\n        targetIndex = (index - 1 + tabs.length) % tabs.length;\n        break;\n      case 'Home':\n        e.preventDefault();\n        targetIndex = 0;\n        break;\n      case 'End':\n        e.preventDefault();\n        targetIndex = tabs.length - 1;\n        break;\n      default:\n        // Do NOT intercept Enter or Space \u2014 native <button> triggers click automatically\n        return;\n    }\n\n    if (targetIndex !== null) {\n      setActiveTab(targetIndex);\n      tabRefs.current[targetIndex]?.focus();\n    }\n  };\n\n  return (\n    <div className=\"tabs-container\">\n      <div \n        role=\"tablist\" \n        aria-label=\"Content tabs\"\n        aria-orientation=\"horizontal\"\n      >\n        {tabs.map((tab, index) => (\n          <button\n            key={tab.id}\n            ref={(el) => (tabRefs.current[index] = el)}\n            id={`tab-${tab.id}`}\n            role=\"tab\"\n            aria-selected={index === activeTab}\n            aria-controls={`panel-${tab.id}`}\n            tabIndex={index === activeTab ? 0 : -1}\n            onClick={() => handleTabClick(index)}\n            onKeyDown={(e) => handleTabKeyDown(e, index)}\n            className={`tab-button ${index === activeTab ? 'active' : ''}`}\n          >\n            {tab.label}\n          </button>\n        ))}\n      </div>\n\n      <div className=\"tab-panels\">\n        {tabs.map((tab, index) => (\n          <div\n            key={tab.id}\n            id={`panel-${tab.id}`}\n            role=\"tabpanel\"\n            aria-labelledby={`tab-${tab.id}`}\n            tabIndex={0}\n            hidden={index !== activeTab}\n            className={`tab-panel ${index === activeTab ? 'active' : ''}`}\n          >\n            <p>{tab.content}</p>\n          </div>\n        ))}\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  );\n};\n\nexport default TabsWidget;\n```\n\n---\n\n## Open Questions (Unscored)\n\n1. *Automatic vs. Manual Activation*: Does the design specification require automatic tab selection upon arrow navigation (as implemented in the remediation above), or manual activation where arrow keys move focus and `Enter`/`Space` activates the tab? (Automatic activation is standard for content tabs; manual is recommended if panel loading involves heavy async network calls).\n",
